    {
     "id": "eqweather_scm_step60_cirrus",
     "description": "Reference: the CLOUDY companion of the step-1410 clear-sky test above, from the same WRF 4.8 em_scm_xy single-column run (GNU gfortran 13.3 REAL*4 build, dt = 60 s, data/eqwefic/scm_ref) at step 60 / physics call 120, 1999-10-22 20:00 UTC -- early afternoon local, a cirrus deck aloft. What makes it the cloudy case: cloud ice reaches 4.64e-5 kg/kg in layers 50-56 and cal_cldfra returns a cloud fraction of exactly 1.0 in seven of them, so the longwave sees an overcast deck and the outgoing longwave falls to 129.7 W/m^2 against 256.0 at step 1410. At step 1410 every condensate species is zero and the cldfra assertion is satisfied trivially by both sides being zero; here it is not. THE COLUMN IS ICE-ONLY: q_c, q_r, q_s and q_g are identically zero at this step and the em_scm_xy sounding never produces them anywhere in the 59-hour run, so this test exercises WSM6's ice branch -- deposition and sublimation, ice nucleation, aggregation to snow and ice fallout -- and pins the warm-rain, melting/freezing and graupel branches at zero. The liquid and mixed-phase regimes are covered on the em_quarter_ss supercell column by couplings/microphysics_column.esm and microphysics_column_mixed.esm. THE MICROPHYSICAL REFERENCE is the driver-level dump esm_dump_subassembly_mp_60.json: WSM6's own increment over the model step, (q_out - q)/dt and (th_out - th)/dt with dt = 60 s. That interval is also WSM6's sub-step -- the scheme takes ndt = max(nint(dt/dtcldcr), 1) sub-steps with dtcldcr = 120 s, so ndt = 1 and dtcld = dt = 60 s -- which is why mp_dtcld0 is 60 s and the document's tendency is compared with the increment WRF actually applies divided by the same interval, with no dt-limit extrapolation. Two consequences are visible in the tolerances. (1) Sedimentation: WSM6 moves the falling species with a semi-Lagrangian PLM update while sedimentation.esm exposes the donor-cell flux divergence, and the two agree only as dt -> 0 (see the component's own description); in this column only cloud ice falls, and that is the larger part of the mp_dqi_dt residual. (2) Real32 quantization: th is ~312 K in the cirrus layers, where single precision spaces values 3.05e-5 K apart, so the reference (th_out - th)/dt is quantized at 5.1e-7 K/s and most of its 59 entries are exactly 0 or a small multiple of 2.5e-6 K/s; mp_dtheta_dt is bounded accordingly. NOT REPEATED FROM THE STEP-1410 TEST: dtheta_m_dt_phys, dtheta_m_conv_delta and dtheta_m_dt. Those three are asserted there against WRF's conv_t_tendf_to_moist, which converts the RADIATION and PBL tendencies only -- WRF applies microphysics directly to th and the moist arrays after the RK loop, outside the tendency path -- whereas this document, being a continuous formulation, hands the moist-theta conversion every physics tendency including the microphysical one. The two agree only when microphysics is inactive, which is the step-1410 case and not this one. rth_phys_sum, which is radiation plus PBL alone, IS asserted here and is the WRF-comparable quantity. The dynamics half is unchanged by the cloud and is asserted in full anyway (du_dt_dyn, dv_dt_dyn, dw_dt_nonpg, dw_dt, dtheta_m_dt_dyn, p_p, and the du_dt/dv_dt sums), from the same dumped mass-coupled increments divided by the coupling mass of their grid point as at step 1410; the formulas were checked against the step-1410 assertions and reproduce them exactly. Everything else -- the 24 surface-layer outputs, the PBL profile and tendencies, the RRTM fluxes and heating, Dudhia, the slab, and the cloud fraction -- comes from the same dump variables as the step-1410 assertions, at step 60 / call 120, and carries that test's tolerances unless stated otherwise, because the residual of a code path does not change with the column.\n TOLERANCES, and what they mean. Measured residuals against the references above (zero-tolerance run, EarthSciAST PR #439 binary): the dynamics tendencies 1.2e-10, 2.5e-11, 3.4e-11, 2.2e-4 and 2.7e-10, p_p 0.018 Pa, the surface layer <= 7.6e-6 relative, the PBL profile 0.029 and 0.019 m^2/s in K_h and K_m and <= 5.7e-5 in the four tendencies, the RRTM fluxes 2.3e-4 and 1.3e-4 W/m^2, the heating rates <= 4.0e-9 K/s, the slab <= 9.3e-4 relative -- every one of them inside the step-1410 tolerances, which are therefore carried over unchanged. Four are not. (1) pbl_dqidt was bounded at abs 1e-17 at step 1410 because the column carried no ice for YSU to diffuse; here it does, and the residual is 2.4e-12, so the bound is 2e-11. (2) cldfra was asserted at abs 0 at step 1410 because both sides were identically zero; with the Randall fit live the residual is 9.5e-6, which is the single-precision cancellation in the Randall denominator that cal_cldfra.esm's own description measures at up to 5.1e-5, so the bound is 1e-4. (3) lsm_dTs_dt and lsm_hfx sat within a factor of 1.03 and 1.08 of their step-1410 bounds, too close to be a bound at all, and are loosened to 5e-5 K/s and 3e-3 relative. (4) THE THREE MICROPHYSICAL TENDENCIES DO NOT AGREE WITH WRF AND THEIR TOLERANCES RECORD THAT, they do not certify it: mp_dqv_dt 1.1e-8 against a reference whose own maximum is 3.3e-8, mp_dqi_dt 2.8e-8 against 2.2e-8, mp_dtheta_dt 4.3e-5 K/s against 1.2e-4. The cause is measured, not guessed. mp_melt_dqi_dt, mp_rate_dqi_dt and mp_sed_dqi_dt split D(q_i)|mp into its three stages and come out at exactly 0, 2.15e-8 and 5.30e-8 kg/kg/s: nothing melts or freezes at these temperatures, the deposition and sublimation block is the same size as WRF's whole ice tendency, and SEDIMENTATION ALONE IS 2.4 TIMES WRF'S WHOLE ICE TENDENCY. That is the gap sedimentation.esm states about itself -- WSM6 moves the falling species with a semi-Lagrangian PLM update (nislfv_rain_plm), this component exposes the donor-cell flux divergence, and the two differ by factors of 0.4 to 1.6 and agree only as dt -> 0 -- appearing for the first time in a coupled document, because step 1410 has no falling ice to disagree about. Closing it needs either a PLM-equivalent flux rule in EarthSciDiscretizations or a dt -> 0 extrapolated reference of the kind couplings/microphysics_column.esm's sedimentation tests already use; it is NOT closed here, and the three bounds above are the measurement, not a pass. The vapour and heating residuals are not explained by sedimentation, which moves no vapour and carries no heat; they are the operator-splitting difference -- WRF applies fallout, then melting, then the rate block, then the adjustment, each to the state the previous one left, while this document is a continuous formulation that evaluates every stage at one instantaneous state and sums -- and that difference is O(dtcld) by construction.",
